<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bosniaks</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bosniaks are a South Slavic people, mainly Muslim, native to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandzak">Sandžak</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>. Their identity took shape under Ottoman rule, during which much of the population converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Recognised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> as a nation under the name Muslims from 1968, they adopted the name Bosniaks in 1993 and are the largest group in Bosnia and Herzegovina.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bosniaks.jpg" alt="Bosniaks" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The authors of the final version of the officially adopted flag are Prof. Dr. Enver Imamović and graphic designer Zvo... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bosnia_and_Herzegovina_(1992%E2%80%931998).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> settled the territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a> after the sixth century, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/migration-period">Migration Period</a>, as small tribal units of the Slavic group the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> called the Sclaveni. The first mention of a Bosnian entity, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-administrando-imperio">De Administrando Imperio</a> of about 960, calls <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> a small land belonging to Byzantium, a geographical rather than a national entity. By the 12th century Bosnia had become independent of Hungarian overlordship under Kulin Ban, who ruled from 1180 to 1204.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> lay between Catholic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> areas, with rugged terrain and poor communications, and it developed its own church, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnian-church">Bosnian Church</a>, which both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> condemned as heretical. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> invaded Bosnia between 1235 and 1241 but withdrew. Scholars disagree on whether the Bosnian Church was dualist; John Fine stresses evidence that it kept basic Catholic theology.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the Ottoman conquest of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> in 1463 a distinct community of Bosnian Muslims began to form as the local Slavic population embraced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Mass conversion came in the 1480s in central Bosnia and later elsewhere, at its fullest in the 16th century. At its height Muslims were up to three-fourths of the population, but wars and plagues later reduced them, since they lived in densely populated towns and had to serve in Ottoman campaigns. In the late 17th and early 18th centuries around 130,000 Muslim refugees arrived from Ottoman lands lost in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>.</p><p>Under Ottoman rule life was structured along religious lines, and the term Bosniak was mainly a territorial designation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> promoted a non-confessional unitary Bosniak identity, but only a small circle of Muslim notables favoured it, among them Mehmed Kapetanović. In the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and Slovenes Bosnian Muslims were not acknowledged as a distinct ethnic group. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnian-war">Bosnian War</a> they were subjected to ethnic cleansing and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>, and hundreds of thousands fled. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayton-agreement">Dayton Agreement</a> recognises Bosniaks as one of the three constituent nations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Most Bosniaks speak the Bosnian language, a variety of Serbo-Croatian mutually intelligible with Croatian and Serbian, with more loanwords from Ottoman Turkish, Arabic and Persian. They are traditionally and predominantly Sunni Muslim, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> orders such as the Naqshbandiyya and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadiriyya">Qadiriyya</a> have had an important part. Traditional culture includes the melancholic folk songs called sevdalinka. Their traditional symbol is a coat of arms with six golden lilies, derived from that of the medieval Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bosniaks” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Efforts in 1991 to bring the Socialist Republic of Bosnia and Herzegovina into the federation, through talks between Milošević, Bosnia's Serbian Democratic Party and the Bosniak vice-president Adil Zulfikarpašić, ended by late 1991, when Izetbegović planned a referendum on independence and violence broke out between Serbs and <b>Bosniaks</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Partisans led by Josip Broz Tito, joined by Croats, Serbs, <b>Bosniaks</b> and others, soon controlled much of the NDH, leaving garrison towns in a de facto state of siege; in 1944 Croats formed 61% of the Partisan units from the Federal State of Croatia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/independent-state-of-croatia">Independent State of Croatia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>It brought together Slovenes, Croats, Serbs, <b>Bosniaks</b>, Montenegrins and Macedonians under one government for the first time.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
